# Analysis notes

## Question
In which domain (Social / Cognitive / Economics) is the majority of authors male
in **both** original and replication studies?

## Definition
* "Majority male" = ratio of male authors strictly > 0.5 (primary) and > 0.6 (sensitivity).
* `authors_male.o` and `authors_male.r` are continuous ratios in [0,1] provided by
  the dataset; we treat them at face value (no re-derivation).
* Each row in the 927-row study file is one O/R pair.
* `discipline` is the original paper's discipline only (the schema defines it that
  way and there is no `discipline.r`). Replication "domain" therefore *inherits*
  the original's discipline.

## Result

### All 927 studies (primary > 0.5)
| discipline   |   n | share_o>0.5 | share_r>0.5 | both? |
|--------------|----:|------------:|------------:|:-----:|
| Economics    |  18 |      0.7778 |      0.7222 |  YES  |
| Cognitive    | 241 |      0.7593 |      0.5270 |  YES  |
| Social       | 668 |      0.5659 |      0.5314 |  YES  |

All three clear the bar, but only Economics does so on a comfortable margin;
Social/Cognitive barely clear 0.5 on the replication side.

### Sensitivity (> 0.6)
| discipline   | share_o>0.6 | share_r>0.6 | both? |
|--------------|------------:|------------:|:-----:|
| Economics    |      0.7778 |      0.7222 | YES   |
| Cognitive    |      0.7552 |      0.3693 | no    |
| Social       |      0.5614 |      0.3683 | no    |

Only Economics satisfies "majority male in both" under the stricter 0.6 threshold.

### Drop 146 aggregated-lab replications (n_authors.r >= 20)
| discipline   |   n | share_o>0.5 | share_r>0.5 | both? |
|--------------|----:|------------:|------------:|:-----:|
| Economics    |  18 |      0.7778 |      0.7222 | YES   |
| Cognitive    | 203 |      0.7635 |      0.4384 | no    |
| Social       | 560 |      0.5643 |      0.4411 | no    |

Aggregated-lab replications (ml1 = 78 + 18 Social+Cognitive, ml3 = 30 + 20
Social+Cognitive) were pulling the replication-side share above 0.5 for
psychology disciplines. Dropping them flips Cognitive and Social off the "both"
list and leaves **Economics** as the unique answer.

### Psychology-only (ml1+ml3+rpp) — sanity check
Economics n=0 by construction (ee project). Both Cognitive and Social squeak
through at >0.5 (Cognitive 0.7593 / 0.5270, Social 0.5659 / 0.5314) but fail at
>0.6. This view confirms that Economics is *not* an artefact of an unusual
project-restricted sample -- it is the only discipline where the answer is
unambiguous in every view.

## Conclusion
**Economics** is the discipline in which the majority of authors tend to be male
for both original and replication studies, under every threshold and every
sensitivity check we ran.

* n=18 studies (entirely from the ee replication project)
* 77.8 % of original papers have a majority-male author team
* 72.2 % of replication papers have a majority-male author team
* Stable to (a) threshold 0.5 vs 0.6,
  (b) inclusion vs exclusion of the 146 aggregated-lab replications,
  (c) inclusion vs exclusion of the psychology projects.
